30 Minutes Human # 00050
Toddler, Crying, Juice Spill, Woman, Calm Down
September 28, 2025

I didn't know what to say. Who the hell does? What's the right line when you're staring at a little someone whose life, as far as they know at that moment, has just been irrevocably shattered?
My mom would've known what to say. The same applies to my aunt and my grandmother, and perhaps to their respective men as well. I, however, had no idea. Maybe that's because I had no kids of my own, though perhaps even with kids I'd not know what to do.
The look on her face was  heartbreaking. She stared down at the red puddle and at the shards of glass with a grimace on her face that would've stopped a bullet train  in its tracks.
Then began a squeal, a scream, a wail, a cry, and a lament—all in one—that terrified me. If before I'd known confusion, now I knew terror. How does one silence a crying child?
"Hey now. Hey. It's okay. Alicia, hey."
I squatted down and patted her on the head. I patted lightly, but hard enough for her to know that I was there.
"Look, it's alright. It's just juice, right? Just drink. Drinky-drink." 'Drinky-drink? What the freakin'...
The muscles on her face had contorted to something almost indistinguishable. I needed to cheer this kid up quick, to take her away from here. That was it! I scooped her up as I best could and looked toward the corner table where her parents sat. The mess on the floor could be somebody else's problem.
Alas, not only were her parents not there, but she wasn't having it. As soon as I tried to cradle her (how does one learn to hold a child?), she screamed even harder and squirmed like I never imagined a human being could. She was out of my arms in under three seconds, and if I hadn't stooped right back down, she would've fallen to the floor, gravity be praised.
Alicia stared at the puddle, stared at the glass, and screamed even louder.
"We'll get you some more! Don't worry about it, honey. Oh, honey. Come here." I reached out for her, and she swatted my hand away.
Why did people engender these little devil-creatures, anyway? I thought that they were only bad when they were teenagers; this was a nightmare of proportions hitherto undreamt of. What would Alicia be like when she was 17? Forget this, I'd be getting my tubes immediately.
People were starting to look around, and a couple of the older couples seemed annoyed. How dare a child weep at an event like this? I don't know what anybody was thinking when bringing youngsters under the age of 45 here; the nerve of it!
She was still standing there. She now resembled a small statue, arms straight down, fists balled, head slightly tilted forward so as to give her a clear perspective of the casualty on the floor. Screaming, of course. I glanced back toward the table with the drinks, but as indicated by the bartender, there was no more red juice. Only yellow juice. The last of the red juice had just died an ignominious death in the middle of what might later become a dance floor.
"Hey, little one. Hi! What's your name? What did you spill?"
She sounded like Mrs. Doubtfire, though when I turned around I saw that she looked like a kinder version of Elizabeth Warren. She was kneeling by Alicia, speaking in a calm voice that I would've thought Alicia couldn't hear... but such suspicious often have a way of being proved unfounded.
"Was that your juice? Your red juice? Did it spill? I'm sorry."
There was the briefest pause in the wailing. This was very impressive. I was surprised to find myself riveted while I eavesdropped on my ward's conversation.
"Would you like some more juice? Don't worry about it. Come, let's find some more juice. Do you like red juice?"
Alicia glanced at the woman. "Yes," she said as she nodded between sobs.
"Let's go find you some red juice, then," said the woman. "I'm Emily," she added.
Emily stood and offered her hand to the weeping toddler, and the hand was taken. They began to walk off towards the drinks table.
"There isn't, there aren't, the red..." was all I managed before Emily glanced at me, smiled, winked, and continued on her way. I followed dumbly.
When they reached the table, Emily said, "Hello there. I'd like to apologize on behalf of this little lady. She just spilled some red juice."
The bartender glanced at the child and said, "Sure, no problem. Somebody will clean it up." As if on cue, a woman in a grey uniform materialized by one of the doors and marched over to the spill with mop and bucket in hand.
"My little friend here would like more red juice. Could you give her some?"
The guy glanced at me before he responded. "I'm sorry, ma'am, but we just ran out. That was the very last glass."
I could feel the mask on Alicia's face stretching into grimace-mode once again. Emily, you're about to get it, lady.
"Oh, that's a shame," she responded without losing her stride. "What other colors do you have?"
"Ma'am, right now what we have is orange-tangerine-mango." He nodded his head toward a big container full of yellow liquid.
"That looks delicious! I'll have half of a small glass, please."
I noticed that she hadn't looked at Alicia since the conversation began, but that Alicia had been watching the exchange closely. She was still whimpering, but it was now more of a reflexive background thing, than a full-on pre-wail.
Emily received the half-glass and looked at it closely, smiled a big smile, and downed it slowly but in one gulp. "Delicious!" she exclaimed. "Can I have a bit more? Maybe... two thirds of a glass this time, please."
The bartender had nobody else in line, so he humored her. Emily held the glass for a moment, took a sip, and then said almost to herself, "So good, so yummy."
"Lisha, Lisha." Alicia shot up both of her hands and repeated her interest in trying this new yellow concoction—a concoction that she had almost violently refused to taste just a few minutes earlier.
"Oh, you want some?" asked Emily. "Of course! Here you go, sweetheart." She bent over and gave the small glass to the little girl. Alicia eyed it briefly and then took a sip. She tasted, exhaled, and took another sip.
"Is it good?" asked Emily, glancing behind me. I turned and saw that the woman with the mop had just finished and was now heading right back to where she'd emerged from.
"Yes," said Alicia. She took a third sip. "Good."
"Princess, I need to go. You enjoy that juice and have fun, okay?" Emily gave her a light touch on the cheek with the back of her fingers and left, though not before giving me a smile and a nod.
That seemed sorta patronizing, didn't it? No, I didn't have her experience. But she didn't even talk to me, just treated me like I was one more of the cheap items on display.
Though, heck, maybe I was. I'd failed spectacularly and she'd effortlessly put Humpty Dumpty together again. Maybe I was on the wrong end of the accusation gun.
Alicia watched Emily walk away for a few seconds and then looked at me. "Joosh," she said, the small glass held between her two hands. This one was less heavy than the previous one. This one wouldn't fall.
"Right," I responded. "Juice. Do you like it?"
"Yes. Joosh."
"Alright, great. Let's go find your parents." I offered my hand, she took it, and we headed back toward the corner table. Both of us glanced toward the floor, and since neither of us saw the spill nor anything related to it, we continued walking.
If the mess with the red juice had at some point grabbed people's attention, that moment was long-gone. Most of the crowd at the tables barely registered Alicia and I as we wove our way across the hall.

[Author's notes: I drew inspiration from a certain 2-year-old that I'm familiar with, and from how clueless somebody who's never dealt with a kid  can be. Also, how much nodding and pointless action detail is there in this first draft? Perhaps too much, methinks.]
